Notes for people hacking on crabbucket
======================================

Read doc/DESIGN first.  It is the concept and the reasoning behind every
decision below, and it is short.


Layout
------

  crates/crabbucket      the framework: config, content, markdown, url,
                         theme, site
  crates/crabbucket-cli  the `crab' executable, a thin front end
  crates/crabbucket-ui   the default design system; its build.rs
                         generates the token module from its own
                         design/tokens.toml
  crates/crabbucket-theme-plain
                         a second design system, which exists so the
                         seam is a seam and not a habit
  crates/crabbucket-tokens
                         the token generator, and the contrast maths
  crates/crabbucket-routes
                         route derivation, and the `Route' enum a site
                         crate generates
  crates/crabbucket-og   social cards
  examples/site          crabbucket's own site
  examples/site-crate    a site that is a crate: typed routes, its own
                         theme and its own directive
  examples/book-samples  the Rust the book shows, as a crate that is
                         compiled -- a third design system, Ferrite
  bench                  a synthetic site, and the clock


The dev loop
------------

  turborust up

turborust rebuilds the example site when anything it depends on changes,
serves dist/ with live reload, and puts build errors in a browser
overlay.  crabbucket deliberately has no dev server of its own; writing a
second, worse one here would be the least defensible thing in the tree.


The roadmap
-----------

The roadmap and the issues live in the repository, as Markdown files under
`cairn/items', described by the schema in `cairn.toml'.  ROADMAP.md is
generated from them and should not be edited by hand.

  cairn next          what is ready to start
  cairn claim <ID>    take it
  cairn show <ID>     read it in full
  cairn close <ID>    finish it
  cairn check         validate every item against the schema

Do not open a TODO file.  An item that is not in `cairn/items' is not on
the roadmap, and a roadmap that drifts from the work is the rot the tool
exists to prevent.  AGENTS.md says the same thing to coding agents.


Measuring
---------

  cargo run --release -p crabbucket-bench -- generate 500 /tmp/bench
  cargo run --release -p crabbucket-bench -- time /tmp/bench

`generate' writes the same pages three ways -- crabbucket, Hugo and Zola
-- and `time' reports the fastest of three runs for each, plus a
breakdown of crabbucket's four passes.  Hugo and Zola are skipped if they
are not installed.  Release mode, always: a debug build measures rustc.

The numbers, and what they say, are on the Speed page of the site.  They
are not all flattering, and that is the point of having them.

CI runs the third subcommand:

  cargo run --release -p crabbucket-bench -- budget bench-site 2000

which fails if the build is slower than the ceiling.  The ceiling is
loose on purpose.  A shared runner varies by more than any change worth
making, and a budget that fails on noise is a budget people rerun until
it passes.


Making a release
----------------

Seven crates go to crates.io.  They depend on each other, so they are
published in this order, which is the order the release workflow uses:

  crabbucket-routes  crabbucket-tokens  crabbucket-og
  crabbucket
  crabbucket-ui      crabbucket-theme-plain
  crabbucket-cli

`bench' and `examples/site-crate' are `publish = false'.  They are part of
the repository, not of the product.

The checklist:

  1.  Decide the version and set it in [workspace.package], and in
      [workspace.dependencies] where each path dependency also carries a
      `version'.  Cargo will not publish a path dependency without one.
  2.  Move the NEWS section for the release from "(unreleased)" to the
      date, and start a new one above it.
  3.  make check.
  4.  cargo package --workspace --locked, and read the file list of each
      tarball.  `include' is an allow-list; a new directory that should
      ship is a directory nobody added to it.
  5.  Open the pull request.  The Release workflow packages every crate on
      every pull request, so this is checked before the tag exists.
  6.  Merge, then tag the merge commit `vX.Y.Z' and push the tag.  The
      workflow refuses to publish if the tag and the manifest disagree.
  7.  Only after the first publish succeeds, change the install line in
      examples/site/content/docs/getting-started.md from `cargo install
      --git' to `cargo install crabbucket-cli'.  Documenting it earlier
      documents something that does not work yet.

Publishing needs a CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN secret on the `crates-io'
environment, scoped to these seven crates.

Two things that cannot be undone: a name, and a version.  A version can be
yanked, which stops new dependants resolving to it, but it is never
removed and the number is never reusable.  That is why step 4 is reading
rather than skimming.


Testing
-------

  make test        the suite, and the doctests
  make cover       how much of it is covered, with a floor
  make audit       licences and advisories
  make api         whether this branch breaks the public API
  make fuzz        the four parsers, on arbitrary bytes
  make mutants     whether the tests would notice

`make test' runs two commands, because nothing runs both.  `cargo test
--all-targets' silently excludes doctests, and it is the obvious thing to
write -- so every `///' example in the public API went unverified until
that was noticed.

The runner is cargo-nextest, which gives a process per test and therefore
a per-test timeout; `.config/nextest.toml' has the profiles.  It is not
required: without it the Makefile falls back to `cargo test'.

Where a new test goes, in the order to prefer:

  next to the code       a `mod tests' at the foot of the file, for
                         anything that is a property of one function
  crates/*/tests/        an end-to-end test through the public API.
                         crabbucket/tests/build.rs is the important one:
                         a fixture site per gate, asserting that a bad
                         site *fails* and that the message names the file
  crates/crabbucket/tests/sites/
                         those fixtures, one directory each, named after
                         the thing that is wrong with them
  examples/*/tests/      whether the documented thing is still true --
                         that the book's snippets are the sample crate's
                         source, that the second design system still
                         renders somebody else's content

Four kinds of test that are not example-based, and what each is for:

  properties     crates/crabbucket/tests/properties.rs.  A sentence from
                 the documentation, turned into something a machine can
                 try to break.  "Every spelling of a base path normalises
                 to the same thing" cannot be held by four examples.
                 Three bugs came out of writing that file.

  snapshots      A rendered page and the stylesheet, per design system.
                 `contains()' answers "is the thing I thought of still
                 there?"; a snapshot answers "what changed?".

                   cargo insta review

                 A diff that is the change you meant is the point.
                 Accepting one without reading it is the same as not
                 having the test.

  fuzzing        fuzz/, on nightly, outside the workspace because it
                 builds with a sanitizer.  The four parsers all run on
                 whatever is in a content file.  `fuzz/seeds/' is
                 committed; `fuzz/corpus/' is what the fuzzer grows from
                 it and is not.  A crash lands in `fuzz/artifacts/':
                 turn it into a named unit test first, then add the input
                 to the seeds.

  mutants        cargo-mutants changes the code and runs the suite.  A
                 mutant that survives is a change nothing objected to --
                 a missing assertion, or a line that does not matter.
                 The first run of it found that emptying `Url`'s `Render`
                 implementation broke no test, which meant nothing tested
                 the one thing that type exists to do.

A property test tells you something is wrong; a named example test stops
it coming back.  When one fails, fix the code, then add the input it
failed on as an ordinary `#[test]' with a name.

The gates are the product.  "The build either fails, or the site is
correct" is a claim about failure, and a claim about failure that is not
tested is a claim that will quietly stop being true.  A new gate needs a
fixture that fails it, not only a site that passes.

Two things the suite has learned the hard way, both worth not repeating:

  A test that needs a scratch directory needs its *own* scratch
  directory.  Three tests once shared one and each began by deleting it,
  which was green on two platforms and red on the third.

  Reading code on one platform is not a substitute for running it on
  three.  The Windows job has found bugs that careful review did not,
  twice.


Before sending a patch
----------------------

  make check

which is fmt, clippy, the test suite, and a build of the example site.

`make cover', `make audit' and `make api' are not in `make check'.  Each
needs a tool that is a separate installation, and each runs as its own
job in CI, which is where they are enforced.

`make fuzz' and `make mutants' are not in either.  Both are slow enough
that running them on a pull request would mean running them briefly,
and both find nothing when run briefly.  They are Monday jobs.


Style
-----

Rust code is formatted by rustfmt; see rustfmt.toml.  Beyond that:

  - No string where a type will do.  That is the whole project.  A URL is
    a Url, not a String; frontmatter is a struct, not a map; a colour is
    a token constant, not a hex literal.
  - Every build error names the file it came from.  crate::Error makes
    that structural: no variant can be constructed without a path.
  - Diagnostics go to standard error, prefixed with the program name.  A
    usage error exits 2; a build failure exits 1.  --help and --version
    always exit 0.
  - Nothing outside crabbucket-ui's generated token module may contain a
    colour or a length.
  - Every user-visible change gets a NEWS entry, and every change gets a
    ChangeLog entry.
  - A change to anything doc/STABILITY section 1 covers is a major
    version.  Read it before changing a public signature.
  - Three rules for anything added to the public surface, from section 7:
    it is re-exported at the crate root, it prints usefully, and what is
    easy to forget to print is in that output.
  - A change that closes a roadmap item closes it in the same commit.

ChangeLog entries use the customary GNU format: a header line with the
date, author name and address, then a blank line, then a one-line
summary, then the affected files each introduced with a tab and an
asterisk.
